==English==

===Etymology===
From {{bor|en|grc|ἀγωνιστικός}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|en|/aɡəˈnɪstɪk/}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# Of or relating to contests that were originally [[participated]] in by the Ancient Greeks; [[athletic]].<ref>{{w|Thomas Blount (lexicographer)|Thomas Blount}}, ''Glossographia,'' London: George Sawbridge, 1661: ''Agonistic(al):'' “warlike or skilful in exercises.”<sup>[http://name.umdl.umich.edu/A28464.0001.001]</sup></ref>
#* '''1673''', {{w|Theophilus Gale}}, ''A Discourse of Christ’s Coming,'' London: John Hancock Senior and Junior, Chapter{{nbsp}}2, Section{{nbsp}}4, p.{{nbsp}}70,<sup>[http://name.umdl.umich.edu/A41706.0001.001]</sup>
#*: These words, with what follow, are for the most part '''''agonistic''','' referring to the customes of the Grecian exercices, in their games. The words [translated] ''apprehended,'' signifie to ''catch,'' or apprehend the Crown, that hung over the Goal: so also [the word translated] ''perfect,'' is an '''Agonistic''' word that signifies the being ''crowned,'' or ''receiving the reward:''
#* '''1881''', {{w|Walt Whitman}}, “A Song of Joys” in ''{{w|Leaves of Grass}},'' London: David Bogue, p.{{nbsp}}147,<sup>[https://babel.hathitrust.org/cgi/pt?id=uc2.ark:/13960/t12n4zs33&view=1up&seq=155&skin=2021&q1=agonistic]</sup>
#*: Joys of the solitary walk, the spirit bow’d yet proud, the suffering and the struggle?
#*: The '''agonistic''' throes, the ecstasies, joys of the solemn musings day or night?
# {{lb|en|zoology|anthropology}} Characterised by [[conflict]] or [[hostility]].
#* '''1978''', {{w|Donald Symons}}, “The Question of Function: Dominance and Play” in Euclid O. Smith (ed.), ''Social Play in Primates,'' New York: Academic Press, p.{{nbsp}}207,<sup>[https://archive.org/details/socialplayinprim0000anim/page/207/mode/1up?q=agonistic]</sup>
#*: Although most '''agonistic''' interactions consist of signals of threat and submission, symbolic aggression is effective only because the possibility of escalation exists {{...}}
#* '''2006''', {{w|Karen Armstrong}}, ''The Great Transformation'', Atlantic Books 2007, p. 66:
#*: This was clearly such an event, but like the ancient religion of Israel, it was also deeply '''agonistic'''.
#* '''2014''', Lucas Bessire, ''Behold the Black Caiman: A Chronicle of {{w|Ayoreo}} Life,'' The University of Chicago Press, Introduction, p.{{nbsp}}8,
#*: Sociality swung wildly between extremes of collective affiliation and '''agonistic''' striving for dominance.
# {{lb|en|rhetoric}} [[argumentative|Argumentative]]; [[combative]].
#* '''1992''', {{w|Kim Stanley Robinson}}, ''{{w|Mars trilogy|Red Mars}},'' New York: Bantam, 1993, Part{{nbsp}}1, p.{{nbsp}}15,<sup>[https://archive.org/details/redmars0000robi/page/15/mode/1up?q=agonistic]</sup>
#*: Sharply Frank said, “A place like this is a crimp in your plan, isn’t it?”
#*: Boone’s gaze returned to him. The surrounding crowd slipped away, sensing the '''agonistic''' nature of the exchange.
# Struggling to achieve an [[effect]]; [[strained]] and [[contrived]].
# {{lb|en|pharmaceutical effect|biochemistry}} Pertaining to an [[agonist]].

